From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 03 Nov 2026 13:10:38 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: name the Kaanapali PD hard-reset
 and PPS-check notifications

The Kaanapali Oplus charger firmware raises three notifications for a
PD source that the driver does not know: 0x56 when the source carries
the Oplus SVID (an Oplus PD brick), 0x7f on a PD hard reset and 0x91
when its PPS check of the source completed. Each is logged as an
"unknown notification" at error level today (qcom_battmgr.c:1503-1505
at the pin). The stock kernel's glink backend names them BC_PD_SVOOC,
PD_CONNECT_HARD_RESET and PD_PPS_CHECK_COMPLETED (oplus_hal_adsp.h:88,
:117, :119); it marks the source as an Oplus brick on the first
(oplus_hal_adsp.c:3431-3443), signals its reverse-charging IC on the
hard reset and marks its PD check done on the third
(oplus_hal_adsp.c:3591-3601). The stock boot logs captured on the
OnePlus Pad 4 hold 99 battery-changed events (0x80) and none of these:
a USB host was attached in every captured boot, so none of the three
has been seen on this unit yet, with either kernel.

Name them. A hard reset drops the contract and the source comes back
at 5 V, and the PPS check changes what the firmware says about the
source, so both make the USB supply re-read its properties, as the
Type-C and power-role events already do. The SVID event is logged at
info level: whether the owner's PD PPS supply raises it decides
nothing in this driver, but a direct charger's policy for Oplus bricks
does depend on it. Standard charging is unchanged: no property or vote
is written.

Signed-off-by: John Stockdale <jstockdale@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c |   14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
index 1211faefbb58..294706c682c2 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -116,11 +116,14 @@ enum qcom_battmgr_variant {
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_OTG_DISABLE				0x51
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_CID_DETECT				0x53
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_TYPEC_STATE_CHANGE			0x55
+#define NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_SVOOC				0x56
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_PLUGIN_IRQ				0x57
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_APSD_DONE				0x58
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_UFCS_DISABLE_MOS			0x73
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_SOURCECAP_DONE			0x79
 #define NOTIF_OPLUS_POWER_ROLE_STATUS			0x7d
+#define NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_HARD_RESET			0x7f
+#define NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_PPS_CHECK_COMPLETED		0x91
 
 #define BATTMGR_OPLUS_PROPERTY_SET			0x300
 #define BATTMGR_OPLUS_PROPERTY_GET			0x301
@@ -1477,8 +1480,19 @@ static void qcom_battmgr_notification(struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr,
 			return;
 		case NOTIF_OPLUS_TYPEC_STATE_CHANGE:
 		case NOTIF_OPLUS_POWER_ROLE_STATUS:
+		/*
+		 * A PD hard reset drops the contract and the source comes
+		 * back at 5 V; the PPS check tells that the firmware is done
+		 * classifying a PD source. Both change what the supply reports.
+		 */
+		case NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_HARD_RESET:
+		case NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_PPS_CHECK_COMPLETED:
 			power_supply_changed(battmgr->usb_psy);
 			return;
+		case NOTIF_OPLUS_PD_SVOOC:
+			/* The source carries the Oplus SVID: an Oplus PD brick. */
+			dev_info(battmgr->dev, "PD source with the Oplus SVID\n");
+			return;
 		case NOTIF_OPLUS_CID_DETECT:
 		case NOTIF_OPLUS_UFCS_DISABLE_MOS:
 			return;
-- 
2.53.0
